{:edb/db-before-t 1606 :edb/db-after-t 1607 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534919 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:00:42.052Z" 13194139534919 true] [17592186179367 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ acting in the direction $OP$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2$." 13194139534919 false] [17592186179367 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186179367 1145 "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x=x_1$ to $x=x_2$ is given by\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ at a rate of $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ is\n\n$$\nF = ma = 3 \\cdot (1 + 2x^2) = (3 + 6x^2) \\,\\rm{N}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= ∫_{0}^{2}(3 + 6x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= [3x + 2x^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3(2) + 2(2^{3}) - (3(0) + 2(0^{3})) \\\\\n&= 6 + 16 - 0 \\\\\n&= 22J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 false] [17592186179367 1145 "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x=x_1$ to $x=x_2$ is given by\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ at a rate of $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ is\n\n$$\nF = ma = 3 \\cdot (1 + 2x^2) = (3 + 6x^2) \\,\\rm{N}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3x+2{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(2)+2({2}^{3})-(3(0)+2({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =6+16-0 \\\\  & =22\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186179368 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(4t+1)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$, where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x$, and $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}$." 13194139534919 false] [17592186179368 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(4t+1)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186179368 1145 "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$, initial velocity $v_0$, and final velocity $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\nv(t) &= ∫(4t + 1)dt \\\\\nv(t) &= 2t^{2} + t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = 0$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 2(0^2) + 0 + C \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad C = 0\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) = 2t^2 + t\n$$\n\nIn particular, its inital velocity at time\n\n$$\nt = 1\\,\\rm{s}\n$$\n\nand final velocity at time\n\n$$\nt = 2\\,\\rm{s}\n$$\n\nare\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{0} &= v(1) = 2(1^{2}) + 1 = 3m/s, \\\\\nv_{1} &= v(2) = 2(2^{2}) + 2 = 10m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔKE &= \\frac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 10^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3^{2} \\\\\n&= 100 - 9 \\\\\n&= 91J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 false] [17592186179368 1145 "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity $v_0,$ and final velocity $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =2{t}^{2}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 2(0^2) + 0 + C\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nC = 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = 2t^2 + t.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and final velocity at time $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=2({1}^{2})+1=3\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=2({2}^{2})+2=10\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =100-9 \\\\  & =91\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186179369 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^2-2t+3)\\,\\rm{m}$, where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t=3\\,\\rm{s}$." 13194139534919 false] [17592186179369 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^2-2t+3)\\,\\rm{m},$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t=3\\,\\rm{s}.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186179369 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)=t^2-2t+3,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+3) \\\\  & =2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\nv_0 = v(1) =  2(1) -2 = 0\\,\\rm{m/s},\n$$\n\nand its final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\nv_1 = v(3) = 2(3) -2= 4\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186179369 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=t^2-2t+3\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - 2t + 3) \\\\\n&= 2t - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\nv_0 = v(1) = 2(1) -2 = 0\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nand its final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\nv_1 = v(3) = 2(3) -2= 4\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= ΔKE \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot (4)^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 0^{2} \\\\\n&= 8 - 0 \\\\\n&= 8J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 false] [17592186179370 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(x+3)\\,\\rm{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2$, calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4$." 13194139534919 false] [17592186179370 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(x+3)\\,\\rm{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186179370 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=2$ to $x=4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+3x]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+12)-(2+6) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity $v_1$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 12 & ={v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $4\\,\\rm{m/s}.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186179370 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=2$ to $x=4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= ∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}F(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{2}^{4}(x + 3)dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + 3x]_{2}^{4} \\\\\n&= (8 + 12) - (2 + 6) \\\\\n&= 12J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity\n\n$$\nv=2\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nat $x=2$. So, we can find the velocity $v_1$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= \\frac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2} \\\\\n12 &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot v_{1}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2^{2} \\\\\n12 &= v_{1}^{2} - 4 \\\\\nv_{1}^{2} &= 16 \\\\\nv_{1} &= ± 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration.\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $4\\,\\rm{m/s}$." 13194139534919 false] [17592186179707 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319828 13194139534919 true] [17592186179707 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319835 13194139534919 true] [17592186179707 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319842 13194139534919 true] [17592186179707 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319856 13194139534919 true] [17592186179708 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319891 13194139534919 true] [17592186179708 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319898 13194139534919 true] [17592186179708 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319905 13194139534919 true] [17592186179708 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319912 13194139534919 true] [17592186179708 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319919 13194139534919 true] [17592186179709 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319800 13194139534919 true] [17592186179709 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319807 13194139534919 true] [17592186179709 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319814 13194139534919 true] [17592186179709 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319821 13194139534919 true] [17592186179710 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319849 13194139534919 true] [17592186179710 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319863 13194139534919 true] [17592186179710 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319870 13194139534919 true] [17592186179710 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319877 13194139534919 true] [17592186179710 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319884 13194139534919 true] [17592186180030 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534919 true] [17592186180030 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $2x^{2}N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x = 0$ to $x = 1$." 13194139534919 false] [17592186180030 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186180030 1145 "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186180034 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534919 true] [17592186180034 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $2t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186180034 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $2kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $2tm/s$, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t = 1s$ and $t = 2s$." 13194139534919 false] [17592186180034 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=2t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =16-4 \\\\  & =12\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319800 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319801 13194139534919 true] [17592186319800 1089 "q-163671" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319800 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534919 true] [17592186319800 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $(t+1)\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319800 1113 #uuid "8305d2ef-8fb9-5f80-b2fe-3b5b2380f4bf" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319800 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=2+1=3\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=4+1=5\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (5)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319802 13194139534919 true] [17592186319801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319803 13194139534919 true] [17592186319801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319804 13194139534919 true] [17592186319801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319805 13194139534919 true] [17592186319801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319806 13194139534919 true] [17592186319801 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319801 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319801 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319806 13194139534919 true] [17592186319801 1110 #uuid "4e60c184-afc4-5553-a0d0-f418d78f5027" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319802 1034 "25\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319802 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319802 1111 #uuid "34113ccc-051e-5d0b-bd95-a5057d7b8b3b" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319803 1034 "16\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319803 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319803 1111 #uuid "f3d8af87-2f59-5e5b-a6b8-28af47d706e5" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319804 1034 "9\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319804 1111 #uuid "9b6e4cb5-ad07-5494-9efd-5599005987b3" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319805 1034 "17\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319805 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319805 1111 #uuid "ec2aa5ef-c10c-5b26-a07c-c09856ccc90c" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319806 1034 "8\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319806 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319806 1111 #uuid "06100131-f71c-52c1-88e1-67af380ee926" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319807 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319808 13194139534919 true] [17592186319807 1089 "q-163678" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319807 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534919 true] [17592186319807 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(3{t}^{2}+4)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319807 1113 #uuid "f249579b-a99f-5b51-b0c7-e7f72d9a4512" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319807 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=3{t}^{2}+4,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n1=(0)^{3}+4(0)+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}+4t+1.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=(1)^{3}+4(1)+1=6\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=(2)^{3}+4(2)+1=17\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {17}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =289-36 \\\\  & =253\\,J.\\en