^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319830 13194139534919 true] [17592186319829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319831 13194139534919 true] [17592186319829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319832 13194139534919 true] [17592186319829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319833 13194139534919 true] [17592186319829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319834 13194139534919 true] [17592186319829 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319829 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319829 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319830 13194139534919 true] [17592186319829 1110 #uuid "2446c211-e40c-519d-822e-3b3e250f33e7" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319830 1034 "\\frac{3}{4}\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319830 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319830 1111 #uuid "170caac7-537f-5c33-83b6-6c8d6663d3f7" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319831 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319831 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319831 1111 #uuid "aa5e028f-a584-5d04-904f-333a80c1b31c" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319832 1034 "2\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319832 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319832 1111 #uuid "0c2508b5-7dff-50f1-8330-562e0e9ffa88" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319833 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319833 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319833 1111 #uuid "dbc595fd-c05a-5a37-9246-b109b7f52df2" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319834 1034 "3\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319834 1111 #uuid "a61ac1a6-cf1c-5ec6-a812-c65f0b7570db" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319835 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319836 13194139534919 true] [17592186319835 1089 "q-163690" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319835 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534919 true] [17592186319835 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $2.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319835 1113 #uuid "7a3795e2-15db-589d-bc2e-076ba2e315ac" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319835 1145 "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $2.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=2.5\\cdot (4-{x}^{2})=(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[10x-\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(10(1)-\\frac{5}{6}({1}^{3}))-(10(0)-\\frac{5}{6}({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =10-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{6}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319837 13194139534919 true] [17592186319836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319838 13194139534919 true] [17592186319836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319839 13194139534919 true] [17592186319836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319840 13194139534919 true] [17592186319836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319841 13194139534919 true] [17592186319836 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319836 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319836 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319838 13194139534919 true] [17592186319836 1110 #uuid "195f8261-fa3a-5f1a-870b-078352af33ea" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319837 1034 "\\frac{21}{2}\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319837 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319837 1111 #uuid "b98165d9-b7fb-50e1-a888-eaebaea642fe" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319838 1034 "\\frac{55}{6}\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319838 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319838 1111 #uuid "0065a181-cfa5-5478-9178-21cc4a9afcd9" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319839 1034 "9\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319839 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319839 1111 #uuid "19d15234-0fb9-594f-a6f3-a3a88e27c15e" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319840 1034 "\\frac{17}{2}\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319840 1111 #uuid "54875c30-d61b-518a-9d41-e77b69a350f2" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319841 1034 "8\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319841 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319841 1111 #uuid "86a85607-1b37-51f7-8bf1-b88bfb99ddad" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319842 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319843 13194139534919 true] [17592186319842 1089 "q-163693" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319842 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534919 true] [17592186319842 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=e.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319842 1113 #uuid "d2ecb6c9-6f8f-54e1-be19-d2a5115c26c3" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319842 1145 "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $4\\,kg$ at a rate of $(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=4(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})=(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+{e}^{4}-(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =4+{e}^{4}-0-1 \\\\  & =({e}^{4}+3)\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319844 13194139534919 true] [17592186319843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319845 13194139534919 true] [17592186319843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319846 13194139534919 true] [17592186319843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319847 13194139534919 true] [17592186319843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319848 13194139534919 true] [17592186319843 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319843 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319843 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319845 13194139534919 true] [17592186319843 1110 #uuid "5bef13e4-7df2-5e1e-a261-d9bdb99bb8f0" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319844 1034 "4{e}^{4}\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319844 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319844 1111 #uuid "1d61935a-1763-554a-9df1-087fba679411" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319845 1034 "({e}^{4}+3)\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319845 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319845 1111 #uuid "cf5605c7-af67-5dba-af4f-75c72edb12e6" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319846 1034 "4({e}^{4}+1)\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319846 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319846 1111 #uuid "2af63384-cdb9-5d5a-8e0b-39eaf952d327" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319847 1034 "(2{e}^{4}+3)\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319847 1111 #uuid "694d2fa5-b6fb-56d8-8395-ebeb49cee6f2" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319848 1034 "({e}^{4}+1)\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319848 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319848 1111 #uuid "45c05f59-793f-56c7-9345-37eebe567217" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319849 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319850 13194139534919 true] [17592186319849 1089 "q-163694" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319849 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534919 true] [17592186319849 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(8-x)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=3.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319849 1113 #uuid "4f47de47-dd4b-5edd-a08d-f716e491cc12" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319849 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(8-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(24-\\frac{9}{2})-(8-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=3$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =3{v}_{1}^{2}-108 \\\\ 3{v}_{1} & =120 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm \\sqrt{40} \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=3$ is $2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319850 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319851 13194139534919 true] [17592186319850 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319852 13194139534919 true] [17592186319850 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319853 13194139534919 true] [17592186319850 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319854 13194139534919 true] [17592186319850 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319855 13194139534919 true] [17592186319850 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319850 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319850 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319855 13194139534919 true] [17592186319850 1110 #uuid "9a424332-2a32-5e46-afcc-11dd0bbbfac6" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319851 1034 "2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319851 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319851 1111 #uuid "420d7de7-2314-53d6-a1b6-5b8dea09cfb7" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319852 1034 "\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319852 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319852 1111 #uuid "85d0cc88-8c8b-56d2-9a0e-386c1496ef8e" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319853 1034 "\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319853 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319853 1111 #uuid "b84d2e9e-c305-521e-a5bb-4d9b70eac1af" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319854 1034 "2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319854 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319854 1111 #uuid "1ca1a8f0-d408-5e3c-96bc-3f23221b5ac1" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319855 1034 "2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319855 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319855 1111 #uuid "644243de-f3d8-50f1-812d-f35b3a8f984e" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319856 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319857 13194139534919 true] [17592186319856 1089 "q-163695" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319856 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534919 true] [17592186319856 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $3.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $2{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319856 1113 #uuid "d8e7d1ad-f8e5-5653-a444-d370087e763d" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319856 1145 "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $2{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=3.5\\cdot 2{x}^{3}=7{x}^{3}\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}7{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(16-0) \\\\  & =28\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319857 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319858 13194139534919 true] [17592186319857 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319859 13194139534919 true] [17592186319857 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319860 13194139534919 true] [17592186319857 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319861 13194139534919 true] [17592186319857 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319862 13194139534919 true] [17592186319857 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319857 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319857 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319858 13194139534919 tr