ergy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{x}){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}-2) \\\\  & =9\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {3}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319864 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319865 13194139534919 true] [17592186319864 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319866 13194139534919 true] [17592186319864 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319867 13194139534919 true] [17592186319864 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319868 13194139534919 true] [17592186319864 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319869 13194139534919 true] [17592186319864 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319864 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319864 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319866 13194139534919 true] [17592186319864 1110 #uuid "5a3224af-78e6-535d-86ba-a6a8293e3386" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319865 1034 "3\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319865 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319865 1111 #uuid "0e7a3a42-c132-5d2a-aa26-3306c8a7ccdb" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319866 1034 "\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319866 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319866 1111 #uuid "ae588ca3-deff-5082-9d56-92745139b71c" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319867 1034 "\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319867 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319867 1111 #uuid "8e82da4f-02b1-50ae-8dad-b0e02d05102e" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319868 1034 "3\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319868 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319868 1111 #uuid "5da9d34e-ead9-539c-beb4-43005ca82413" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319869 1034 "2\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319869 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319869 1111 #uuid "ab2156ad-e394-55cd-a2ba-9102817ce7aa" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319870 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319871 13194139534919 true] [17592186319870 1089 "q-163698" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319870 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534919 true] [17592186319870 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $7\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(2x+3)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ at $x=3,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." 13194139534919 true] [17592186319870 1113 #uuid "2fcbb421-9d54-5162-95bd-edc60b0635c5" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319870 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(9+9)-(1+3) \\\\  & =14\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ at $x=3.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot (2\\sqrt{5})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 14 & =70-\\frac{7}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =56 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319872 13194139534919 true] [17592186319871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319873 13194139534919 true] [17592186319871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319874 13194139534919 true] [17592186319871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319875 13194139534919 true] [17592186319871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319876 13194139534919 true] [17592186319871 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319871 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319871 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319875 13194139534919 true] [17592186319871 1110 #uuid "e5845577-161c-57a8-be35-c0c1851f5d6c" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319872 1034 "8\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319872 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319872 1111 #uuid "2800b363-8033-54e7-aad0-ab700bad0eac" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319873 1034 "6\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319873 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319873 1111 #uuid "ce47bd81-f9fc-5448-a9d2-96e00e989e89" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319874 1034 "4\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319874 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319874 1111 #uuid "8a7b4e96-e2c4-535a-8bda-3d906a3a02d0" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319875 1034 "4\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319875 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319875 1111 #uuid "8b3ad5ea-871b-5e99-87b1-dece7d2ff0a4" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319876 1034 "\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319876 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319876 1111 #uuid "0b5fbd66-ce0f-5790-8183-b9b8bc751a61" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319877 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319878 13194139534919 true] [17592186319877 1089 "q-163700" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319877 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534919 true] [17592186319877 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $({x}^{3}-4)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=3$, and the particle has velocity $v=10\\,m/s$ at $x=5,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." 13194139534919 true] [17592186319877 1113 #uuid "78314a43-9958-56d6-8eec-89735ec4ff15" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319877 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=3$ to $x=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}({x}^{3}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-4x){|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{625}{4}-20)-(\\frac{81}{4}-12) \\\\  & =128\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=10\\,m/s$ at $x=5.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=3$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =200-2{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2{v}_{0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=3$ is $6\\,m/s.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319878 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319879 13194139534919 true] [17592186319878 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319880 13194139534919 true] [17592186319878 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319881 13194139534919 true] [17592186319878 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319882 13194139534919 true] [17592186319878 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319883 13194139534919 true] [17592186319878 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319878 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319878 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319881 13194139534919 true] [17592186319878 1110 #uuid "c702e0be-4a89-5e91-b790-5bf6d7d350f4" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319879 1034 "2\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319879 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319879 1111 #uuid "dfe6515d-c0e6-5415-b950-5591730d9c27" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319880 1034 "8\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319880 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319880 1111 #uuid "0fa0480e-9467-524b-a695-9a91915c667a" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319881 1034 "6\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319881 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319881 1111 #uuid "5c2073a7-03f1-5a41-8e2e-9d250005a8aa" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319882 1034 "7\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319882 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319882 1111 #uuid "2dc6c981-1087-521c-a58b-28a877111421" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319883 1034 "4\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319883 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319883 1111 #uuid "451e1661-d538-504d-b439-536fe18759ce" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319884 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319885 13194139534919 true] [17592186319884 1089 "q-163702" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319884 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534919 true] [17592186319884 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=2,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319884 1113 #uuid "89e50146-b1c6-571f-ad82-cc9a4f7f02cf" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319884 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8+4-0 \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {7}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{49}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $5\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=7-5=2\\,m/s.\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319885 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319886 13194139534919 true] [17592186319885 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319887 13194139534919 true] [17592186319885 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319888 13194139534919 true] [17592186319885 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319889 13194139534919 true] [17592186319885 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319890 13194139534919 true] [17592186319885 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319885 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319885 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319886 13194139534919 true] [17592186319885 1110 #uuid "dc0b93d0-7729-5089-b09b-93db67a03ee9" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319886 1034 "2\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319886 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319886 1111 #uuid "a0a9d7d4-87d2-523c-86b2-bb1bb56204eb" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319887 1034 "3\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319887 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319887 1111 #uuid "c17366a5-2ffc-5309-bee6-f999ba8ea6ca" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319888 1034 "\\frac{9}{4}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319888 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319888 1111 #uuid "b7430fbf-3739-53db-a6d7-de1ff077c075" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319889 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}\\,m/s" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319889 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592