t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=1\\,s.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319898 1113 #uuid "f7b3785d-8d10-54ef-aace-746016f1c83f" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319898 1145 "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (1+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=0+\\frac{1}{3}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(1)=1+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\frac{16}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319900 13194139534919 true] [17592186319899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319901 13194139534919 true] [17592186319899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319902 13194139534919 true] [17592186319899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319903 13194139534919 true] [17592186319899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319904 13194139534919 true] [17592186319899 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319899 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319899 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319903 13194139534919 true] [17592186319899 1110 #uuid "7002ed18-f578-5c2c-83f1-99cad28e62f7" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319900 1034 "\\frac{8}{3}\\,J"