ond, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{1}{6}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}({1}^{3})=\\frac{1}{6}\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=\\frac{1}{6}({2}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319906 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319907 13194139534919 true] [17592186319906 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319908 13194139534919 true] [17592186319906 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319909 13194139534919 true] [17592186319906 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319910 13194139534919 true] [17592186319906 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319911 13194139534919 true] [17592186319906 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319906 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319906 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319909 13194139534919 true] [17592186319906 1110 #uuid "dad38db8-663f-5e65-9ab8-975723b08fcf" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319907 1034 "4\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319907 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319907 1111 #uuid "77bd545f-33ca-554b-ae98-ede488c8febe" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319908 1034 "\\frac{11}{6}\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319908 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319908 1111 #uuid "38092463-c428-5b54-a9c9-f2312f2aa7ee" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319909 1034 "\\frac{21}{8}\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319909 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319909 1111 #uuid "3842fc37-8450-5bae-8c18-ce70a71d6c45" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319910 1034 "\\frac{15}{4}\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319910 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319910 1111 #uuid "8055d7d2-8273-5a6f-b1d7-0a6554e47d70" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319911 1034 "3\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319911 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319911 1111 #uuid "d8da6c80-68c3-5137-887c-bee64da01315" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319912 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319913 13194139534919 true] [17592186319912 1089 "q-163712" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319912 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534919 true] [17592186319912 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $2{e}^{-t}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s.$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319912 1113 #uuid "ce04dae5-4009-5660-88a7-083f025f0df3" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319912 1145 "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =-2{e}^{-t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=-2{e}^{-0}+C\\,⟹\\,C=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2-2{e}^{-t}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=1\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {(1)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319913 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319914 13194139534919 true] [17592186319913 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319915 13194139534919 true] [17592186319913 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319916 13194139534919 true] [17592186319913 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319917 13194139534919 true] [17592186319913 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319918 13194139534919 true] [17592186319913 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534919 true] [17592186319913 1041 "selection" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319913 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319916 13194139534919 true] [17592186319913 1110 #uuid "a6251978-0f83-5ddf-9b77-7594f945ef7e" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319914 1034 "4\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319914 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319914 1111 #uuid "6189bdf9-d70c-55ca-a0f4-08991a938b25" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319915 1034 "2\\,J" 13194139534919 true] [17592186319915 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534919 true] [17592186319915 1111 #uuid "c1c031c5-537f-5404-9260-c1160